    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_property",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "Apply the property with a = π/2",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Method",
        "margin_note": "First two marks. sinx and cosx swap places under x → π/2 − x, so the denominator does not change.",
        "memory_tip": "sin(π/2 − x) = cosx and cos(π/2 − x) = sinx — the two swap, so sinx + cosx stays sinx + cosx.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Sol:",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          {
            "text": "I = \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} \\sin ^{2}x/(\\sin x + \\cos x) dx \\dots (1)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\text{Use}\\ \\int _{0}^{a}\\ f\\left(x\\right)dx\\ = \\ \\int _{0}^{a}\\ f\\left(a\\ - \\ x\\right)dx,\\ a\\ = \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{2}.",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\text{By}\\ x\\ \\to \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{2} - \\ x:\\ I",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} \\sin ^{2}(\\pi /2 - x)/(\\sin (\\pi /2 - x) +",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\cos (\\pi /2 - x)) dx",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{2} - \\ x\\right)\\ = \\ \\cos x\\ \\text{and}\\ \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{2} - \\ x\\right)\\ = \\ \\sin x:",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\Rightarrow I = \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} \\cos ^{2}x/(\\sin x + \\cos x) dx \\dots (2)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Under x → π/2 − x, sinx and cosx exchange roles, so sin²x turns into cos²x while the denominator sinx + cosx is unchanged (it is symmetric in the two).",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing sin(π/2 − x) as sinx instead of cosx.",
          "Changing the denominator when it in fact stays the same."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_add",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "Add to reach an x-free integral",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Method",
        "margin_note": "Two more marks. sin²x + cos²x = 1 is what makes the numerator collapse.",
        "memory_tip": "sin²x + cos²x = 1 always — the moment you see both squares added, replace them with 1.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Both have the same denominator, so add:",
            "added": true
          },
          "Adding (1) and (2): 2I",
          {
            "text": "= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} (\\sin ^{2}x + \\cos ^{2}x)/(\\sin x + \\cos x) dx",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} dx/(\\sin x + \\cos x)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          "[∵ sin²x + cos²x = 1]",
          {
            "text": "Divide both sides by 2:",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "\\Rightarrow I = (1/2)\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} dx/(\\sin x + \\cos x)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Adding the two forms turns the numerator into sin²x + cos²x, which is identically 1, leaving a single integral with no x in the numerator at all.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Forgetting to replace sin²x + cos²x by 1 and leaving the integral unsimplified.",
          "Dropping the factor of 1/2 when isolating I."
        ],
        "lines_compact": [
          "Adding (1) and (2): 2I",
          {
            "text": "= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} (\\sin ^{2}x + \\cos ^{2}x)/(\\sin x + \\cos x) dx",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} dx/(\\sin x + \\cos x)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          "[∵ sin²x + cos²x = 1]",
          {
            "text": "\\Rightarrow I = (1/2)\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} dx/(\\sin x + \\cos x)",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_reduce",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "Write cosx + sinx as √2 sin(x + π/4)",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Method",
        "margin_note": "Two marks for the R sin(x+α) rewrite and the cosecant integral.",
        "memory_tip": "sinx + cosx = √2 sin(x + π/4) — a standard rewrite, worth memorising outright.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Take √2 out so the bracket becomes a sine:",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "\\sin x\\ + \\ \\cos x\\ = \\ \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\sin x \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\ \\cos x \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\ \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\ = \\ \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\ \\text{so}",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\sin x\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\ + \\ \\cos x\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right)",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin \\left(x\\ + \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right)",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\text{So}\\ \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x\\ + \\ \\cos x} = \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cos ec\\left(x\\ + \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right):",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "I = (1/(2\\sqrt{2}))\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} \\operatorname{cosec} (x + \\pi /4) dx",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\left[\\because \\ \\int \\cos ec\\theta d\\theta \\ = \\ \\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= (1/(2\\sqrt{2}))[\\log \\tan ((x + \\pi /4)/2)]_{0}^{\\pi /2}",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Combining sinx and cosx into a single sine wave R sin(x+α) reduces the integral to a standard cosecant form, whose antiderivative is a known log tan formula.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Forgetting the 1/√2 factors, giving sinx + cosx = sin(x + π/4) with no √2.",
          "Using the wrong standard antiderivative for cosecθ."
        ],
        "lines_compact": [
          {
            "text": "\\sin x\\ + \\ \\cos x\\ = \\ \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\sin x \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\ \\cos x \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin \\left(x\\ + \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{4}\\right)",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "I = (1/(2\\sqrt{2}))\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2} \\operatorname{cosec} (x + \\pi /4) dx",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\left[\\because \\ \\int \\cos ec\\theta d\\theta \\ = \\ \\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= (1/(2\\sqrt{2}))[\\log \\tan ((x + \\pi /4)/2)]_{0}^{\\pi /2}",
            "render": "katex",
            "style": "normal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_final",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Evaluate the log limits",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Solution",
        "margin_note": "The last mark. Rationalising the fraction inside the log turns it into a perfect square, which is what makes the final simplification clean.",
        "memory_tip": "tan(3π/8) = √2 + 1 and tan(π/8) = √2 − 1 — a pair worth memorising for this whole chapter.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "\\text{At}\\ x\\ = \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{2}:\\ \\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\pi }{2} + \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{4}}{2} = \\ \\dfrac{3\\pi }{8}",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "\\text{At}\\ x\\ = \\ 0:\\ \\dfrac{0\\ + \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{4}}{2} = \\ \\dfrac{\\pi }{8}",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\left[\\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\ - \\ \\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\ = \\ \\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\ \\text{and}\\ \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\ = \\ \\sqrt{2}\\ - \\ 1",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1}{\\sqrt{2}\\ - \\ 1}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "Multiply above and below by (√2 + 1):",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\dfrac{\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)^{2}}{\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ - \\ 1\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "(√2 − 1)(√2 + 1) = 2 − 1 = 1:",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)^{2}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\log \\left(p^{2}\\right)\\ = \\ 2\\log p,\\ \\text{and}\\ \\dfrac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\ \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}:",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex",
            "added": true
          },
          {
            "text": "\\therefore \\ I\\ = \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)",
            "style": "boxed",
            "render": "katex"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Rationalising (√2+1)/(√2−1) by multiplying by (√2+1)/(√2+1) turns the denominator into 1, leaving a perfect square inside the log; log(a²) = 2 log(a) turns that square into a factor of 2, which cancels the outer 1/2 exactly.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Leaving the answer as log[(√2+1)/(√2−1)] without rationalising.",
          "Forgetting that log(a²) = 2 log(a), which is what removes the /2 from the coefficient."
        ],
        "lines_compact": [
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\left[\\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)\\ - \\ \\log \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1}{\\sqrt{2}\\ - \\ 1}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\dfrac{\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)^{2}}{\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ - \\ 1\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "= \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left[\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)^{2}\\right]",
            "style": "normal",
            "render": "katex"
          },
          {
            "text": "\\therefore \\ I\\ = \\ \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\log \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\ + \\ 1\\right)",
            "style": "boxed",
            "render": "katex"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
